1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,175
What if you could live
for a thousand years?

2
00:00:06,225 --> 00:00:07,296
Not forever.

3
00:00:07,346 --> 00:00:09,019
Just one thousand years.

4
00:00:09,069 --> 00:00:11,607
Ten full centuries of sunrises, birthdays,

5
00:00:11,657 --> 00:00:12,619
and goodbyes.

6
00:00:12,669 --> 00:00:16,688
It sounds like the greatest
gift anyone could ever receive.

7
00:00:16,738 --> 00:00:19,421
But what would it actually feel like?

8
00:00:19,471 --> 00:00:20,723
Let's find out.

9
00:00:21,571 --> 00:00:23,843
Imagine you were born today.

10
00:00:23,893 --> 00:00:27,178
For the first few decades,
nothing seems unusual.

11
00:00:27,228 --> 00:00:31,024
You go to school, you make
friends, you fall in love,

12
00:00:31,074 --> 00:00:32,810
you find your first job.

13
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:35,728
You grow up just like everyone else.

14
00:00:35,778 --> 00:00:38,275
But somewhere around
your thirtieth birthday,

15
00:00:38,325 --> 00:00:40,384
your body quietly stops aging.

16
00:00:40,434 --> 00:00:45,420
Your heart, your skin, your brain,
all of it stays exactly as it is.

17
00:00:45,470 --> 00:00:47,755
And at first, nobody notices.

18
00:00:47,805 --> 00:00:49,155
Not even you.

19
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:52,799
So how could this even be possible?

20
00:00:52,849 --> 00:00:56,782
In nature, some creatures
already come surprisingly close.

21
00:00:56,832 --> 00:01:00,217
The Greenland shark can live
for around four hundred years,

22
00:01:00,267 --> 00:01:02,771
swimming slowly through
freezing Arctic waters.

23
00:01:02,821 --> 00:01:07,356
A clam nicknamed Ming was found
to be over five hundred years old.

24
00:01:07,406 --> 00:01:12,462
And a tiny jellyfish called Turritopsis
can turn back into a younger version of

25
00:01:12,512 --> 00:01:14,912
itself when it's injured or stressed.

26
00:01:14,962 --> 00:01:19,357
For you, the secret would be cells that
repair themselves almost perfectly,

27
00:01:19,407 --> 00:01:21,912
fixing damage before it builds up.

28
00:01:21,962 --> 00:01:24,656
Your body would simply stop wearing out.

29
00:01:26,604 --> 00:01:28,467
But here's the first surprise.

30
00:01:28,517 --> 00:01:32,035
Even if you never grow
old, you are not immortal.

31
00:01:32,085 --> 00:01:33,658
You can still get sick.

32
00:01:33,708 --> 00:01:35,571
You can still have accidents.

33
00:01:35,621 --> 00:01:39,376
Some scientists have estimated that
if aging disappeared completely,

34
00:01:39,426 --> 00:01:43,861
the average person would still only
live for about a thousand years before

35
00:01:43,911 --> 00:01:45,527
something unlucky happened.

36
00:01:45,577 --> 00:01:48,122
So every single day, for ten centuries,

37
00:01:48,172 --> 00:01:50,035
you'd have to be careful.

38
00:01:50,085 --> 00:01:51,268
Very careful.

39
00:01:54,791 --> 00:01:56,166
Now let's jump ahead.

40
00:01:56,216 --> 00:01:59,215
You're eighty years old,
but you still look thirty.

41
00:01:59,265 --> 00:02:00,830
Your friends are retiring.

42
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:02,520
Their hair has turned grey.

43
00:02:02,570 --> 00:02:04,040
Their steps are slower.

44
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:05,655
People start asking questions.

45
00:02:05,705 --> 00:02:07,441
Doctors want to study you.

46
00:02:07,491 --> 00:02:09,756
Strangers stare at you in the street.

47
00:02:09,806 --> 00:02:14,842
So you make a choice that you'll have
to make again and again for the rest of

48
00:02:14,892 --> 00:02:15,735
your life.

49
00:02:15,785 --> 00:02:18,985
You move to a new city, take a new name,

50
00:02:19,035 --> 00:02:20,277
and start over.

51
00:02:24,373 --> 00:02:26,796
By your one hundred
and twentieth birthday,

52
00:02:26,846 --> 00:02:29,436
everyone you grew up with is gone.

53
00:02:29,486 --> 00:02:32,201
Your parents, your teachers,
your childhood friends.

54
00:02:32,251 --> 00:02:36,819
Maybe even your own children have grown
old and passed away while you stayed

55
00:02:36,869 --> 00:02:37,380
young.

56
00:02:37,430 --> 00:02:41,038
This is the hardest part of
living for a thousand years.

57
00:02:41,088 --> 00:02:43,019
You never stop loving people.

58
00:02:43,069 --> 00:02:44,916
You just keep losing them.

59
00:02:47,259 --> 00:02:51,270
Some long-lived people might choose
to stay away from close relationships

60
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:52,129
altogether.

61
00:02:52,179 --> 00:02:57,381
Others might decide that a few decades
of love is worth any amount of pain.

62
00:02:57,431 --> 00:03:02,290
Either way, you would become an expert
in something nobody wants to master.

63
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:03,346
Saying goodbye.

64
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:07,707
But there are amazing parts too.

65
00:03:07,757 --> 00:03:09,250
Let's talk about money.

66
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:13,606
If you put just one thousand dollars
in the bank and earned five percent

67
00:03:13,656 --> 00:03:17,920
interest every year, after two hundred
years you'd have more than seventeen

68
00:03:17,970 --> 00:03:18,924
million dollars.

69
00:03:18,974 --> 00:03:22,215
After three hundred years,
you'd have over two billion.

70
00:03:22,265 --> 00:03:24,245
Patience becomes your superpower.

71
00:03:24,295 --> 00:03:28,773
You could plant a forest and actually
walk through it when the trees are fully

72
00:03:28,823 --> 00:03:29,314
grown.

73
00:03:29,364 --> 00:03:32,636
You could learn every instrument,
speak dozens of languages,

74
00:03:32,686 --> 00:03:37,048
and earn a degree in every subject
you've ever been curious about.

75
00:03:41,357 --> 00:03:44,204
And speaking of languages,
here's something wild.

76
00:03:44,254 --> 00:03:48,357
The world around you would slowly start
speaking a language you don't fully

77
00:03:48,407 --> 00:03:49,104
understand.

78
00:03:49,154 --> 00:03:54,005
English from a thousand years ago is
almost impossible for us to read today.

79
00:03:54,055 --> 00:03:56,988
It looks like a completely
different language.

80
00:03:57,038 --> 00:03:59,795
So over the centuries,
the way people talk,

81
00:03:59,845 --> 00:04:04,335
the slang, even the meaning of everyday
words would drift away from you.

82
00:04:04,385 --> 00:04:09,169
Every hundred years or so, you'd basically
have to learn your own language all

83
00:04:09,219 --> 00:04:10,315
over again.

84
00:04:13,835 --> 00:04:17,366
By your three hundredth year,
the world is unrecognizable.

85
00:04:17,416 --> 00:04:21,893
Think about how much changed between
the year seventeen twenty-six and today.

86
00:04:21,943 --> 00:04:25,805
Back then, there were no
cars, no electricity in homes,

87
00:04:25,855 --> 00:04:27,551
no phones, no planes.

88
00:04:27,601 --> 00:04:31,507
Now imagine three hundred more
years of change after today.

89
00:04:31,557 --> 00:04:35,339
Cities might float on oceans
or rise high into the clouds.

90
00:04:35,389 --> 00:04:38,394
People might live on the Moon or on Mars.

91
00:04:38,444 --> 00:04:43,113
Maybe you'd be one of the first humans
to see Earth from another planet,

92
00:04:43,163 --> 00:04:46,488
and still have seven
hundred years left to live.

93
00:04:48,985 --> 00:04:51,754
But there's a problem
inside your own head.

94
00:04:51,804 --> 00:04:54,573
Your brain can only hold so many memories.

95
00:04:54,623 --> 00:04:58,504
Even today, you don't remember
most of the days you've lived.

96
00:04:58,554 --> 00:05:01,993
Over a thousand years, you
would forget almost everything.

97
00:05:02,043 --> 00:05:04,251
The face of your first best friend.

98
00:05:04,301 --> 00:05:06,335
The sound of your mother's voice.

99
00:05:06,385 --> 00:05:08,323
The house you grew up in.

100
00:05:08,373 --> 00:05:10,893
To protect your past,
you might keep diaries,

101
00:05:10,943 --> 00:05:12,374
recordings, and photos.

102
00:05:12,424 --> 00:05:13,552
Thousands of them.

103
00:05:13,602 --> 00:05:16,035
Your memories would turn into a library,

104
00:05:16,085 --> 00:05:20,257
and you'd be the only one who
could ever truly understand it.

105
00:05:24,498 --> 00:05:26,465
And what about your mind itself?

106
00:05:26,515 --> 00:05:30,967
Some psychologists believe that a lot
of the meaning in our lives comes from

107
00:05:31,017 --> 00:05:32,912
knowing our time is limited.

108
00:05:32,962 --> 00:05:35,452
We rush to see the people we love.

109
00:05:35,502 --> 00:05:39,318
We chase our dreams because
one day we won't be able to.

110
00:05:39,368 --> 00:05:41,699
But with a thousand years ahead of you,

111
00:05:41,749 --> 00:05:43,716
would you still feel that urgency?

112
00:05:43,766 --> 00:05:46,558
Or would you keep putting
things off, telling yourself,

113
00:05:46,608 --> 00:05:48,452
I'll do it next century?

114
00:05:52,797 --> 00:05:55,819
To stay sane, you'd
probably live in chapters.

115
00:05:55,869 --> 00:05:58,035
One hundred years as a doctor.

116
00:05:58,085 --> 00:06:00,350
One hundred years as a musician.

117
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:02,764
One hundred years exploring the oceans.

118
00:06:02,814 --> 00:06:06,098
One hundred years building
a home on another world.

119
00:06:06,148 --> 00:06:09,366
Each life would feel almost
like a separate person,

120
00:06:09,416 --> 00:06:11,158
connected only by you.

121
00:06:13,741 --> 00:06:18,269
By your five hundredth year, you
have seen empires rise and fall.

122
00:06:18,319 --> 00:06:21,778
You've watched history being
written, and then rewritten.

123
00:06:21,828 --> 00:06:26,449
People would read about events in their
history books that you remember living

124
00:06:26,499 --> 00:06:27,039
through.

125
00:06:27,089 --> 00:06:30,805
You might be the last living
witness to entire eras.

126
00:06:30,855 --> 00:06:32,701
Historians could interview you.

127
00:06:32,751 --> 00:06:36,465
Or you could stay silent and
keep your secret forever.

128
00:06:37,772 --> 00:06:40,778
By your eight hundredth
year, maybe you're tired.

129
00:06:40,828 --> 00:06:43,541
Or maybe you're more curious than ever.

130
00:06:43,591 --> 00:06:48,096
Nobody knows how a human mind would
really feel after this much time.

131
00:06:48,146 --> 00:06:50,013
It's never happened before.

132
00:06:50,063 --> 00:06:53,340
You might become the wisest
person on the planet.

133
00:06:53,390 --> 00:06:54,788
Or the loneliest.

134
00:06:56,758 --> 00:06:59,460
And then, one day, you reach it.

135
00:06:59,510 --> 00:07:01,287
Your one thousandth birthday.

136
00:07:01,337 --> 00:07:03,714
The year is three thousand twenty-six.

137
00:07:03,764 --> 00:07:06,788
You stand somewhere
quiet, maybe on a hill,

138
00:07:06,838 --> 00:07:10,560
maybe on another planet,
and you watch the sunrise.

139
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:14,596
It's the same Sun that rose
on your very first morning.

140
00:07:14,646 --> 00:07:19,063
You've seen it more than three
hundred and sixty-five thousand times.

141
00:07:19,113 --> 00:07:21,517
And somehow, it's still beautiful.

142
00:07:22,356 --> 00:07:25,183
So, would living for a
thousand years be a gift,

143
00:07:25,233 --> 00:07:26,272
or a curse?

144
00:07:26,322 --> 00:07:28,201
Maybe a little bit of both.

145
00:07:28,251 --> 00:07:29,540
But here's the thing.

146
00:07:29,590 --> 00:07:32,497
Whether you live for a
thousand years or for eighty,

147
00:07:32,547 --> 00:07:35,694
you can only ever live one day at a time.

148
00:07:35,744 --> 00:07:37,771
And today is one of them.

149
00:07:41,698 --> 00:07:43,466
So call someone you love.

150
00:07:43,516 --> 00:07:44,678
Learn something new.

151
00:07:44,728 --> 00:07:45,890
Watch the sunrise.

152
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:49,779
Because a long life isn't just
about how many years you have.

153
00:07:49,829 --> 00:07:52,067
It's about what you do with them.

154
00:07:54,694 --> 00:07:55,772
Thanks for watching.

155
00:07:55,822 --> 00:07:58,238
And see you in the next "what if."
